      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "tbrasselle",
        "email": "tbrasselle@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-23T03:48:53Z"
      },
      "message": "feat(wire): closing overlap, centre-line slit and open-run skims for Wire Contour\n\nWire Contour gains a closing overlap (#3216): the ride continues past\nthe seam by the stated arc length before the lead-out back to the\nthreading point, so the start of the cut is burnt over and no witness\nmark is left. A skim inherits it. It is refused by name where there is\nno seam to close past - with tabs (tabs pin the seam) and on an open\nrun - and a negative length is refused at the door.\n\nThe new Centre side cuts an open run as a centre-line slit: the wire\nrides the run itself, the kerf burns both sides, no side is kept, and\nthe kerf law has no product to protect (said on the report line, never\nsilent). Open runs now take skim passes too: each re-enters from the\nair at the run's start end and is walked in the run's own direction; a\nreversed pass is refused by name.\n\nThe project file reads the new fields with serde defaults, so every\nfile written before this reads unchanged. The wire page offers the\noverlap row and the centre side, and the verify judges a centre slit\nas an open run whose ends' clearance is still checked.\n\nFixes #3216",
      "tree": {
